<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stephen II Kotromanić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1353</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen II Kotromanić was ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> from 1322 to 1353. He enlarged the banate greatly, taking Hum (Zachlumia) around 1326 and lands towards the Adriatic, and sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ston">Ston</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peljesac">Pelješac</a> to Ragusa in 1333. He welcomed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>, and his daughter Elizabeth married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stephen-ii-kotromanic.jpg" alt="Stephen II Kotromanić" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Denkmäler der Kunst in Dalmatien, Herausgegeben von Georg Kowalczyk, mit einer Einleitung von Cornelius Gurlitt. Auto... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relikvi%C3%A1%C5%99_sv._Simeona_detail.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
